WHAT THIS COVERS

"The Software" above means everything in this project that is ours: the ETL
pipeline, the Astro site, the components, the scripts and the checks, AND the
four datasets this project codes itself, AND the written explanations on the
site.

The four datasets are:

  - the AI Governance Readiness Index
  - the AI Law and Policy Index
  - the Frontier Safety Framework Index
  - the Compute Threshold Index

One licence for all of it, on purpose. These datasets are not collected, they
are written: each one is a reading of primary legal instruments, coded against
a published rubric, and the code and the coding rules are inseparable in
practice. Splitting them across two licences would invite the question of where
one ends and the other begins, and MIT's terms - use it, change it, sell it,
keep the notice - are the same permission we would have granted anyway. There
is precedent in our own source registry: Microsoft's AI Diffusion dataset, one
of this project's inputs, is MIT-licensed data.

Attribution strings for each dataset are in data/sources.json and are shown in
the provenance marker attached to every figure on the site.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER

Most figures on the site are reproduced from other people's datasets under
their own licences and remain theirs. Those licences are not ours to widen.
Every source, with its licence, its attribution string and its known
limitations, is registered in data/sources.json and listed at
https://aisafetytracker.org/sources/.

Two that come up often: the AI Incident Database is ODbL, which puts
share-alike conditions on derived databases; news headlines and extracts belong
to their publishers, and this project stores a headline, at most 200 characters
and a canonical link.
